WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.402
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-winning

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.937
writer and director
whose films include

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:07.073
"Inglourious Basterds,"
"Kill Bill," and "Pulp Fiction."

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:10.109
His book, "Cinema Speculation,"
is on sale now.

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:13.413
Please welcome back to the show
Quentin Tarantino, everybody.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.215
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:31.831
Welcome back.
-Good to be back.

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.699
-This is a wonderful book
because it is both

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:36.268
a collection of
film criticism about movies

00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:37.670
that are important to you,
but it also has this

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.539
great personal element
about you falling in love

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:42.274
with movies as a kid.

00:00:42.274 --> 00:00:44.410
And a lot of the movies
were introduced to you

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.545
at an age-inappropriate time.

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:49.482
-Well, "inappropriate"
is a fluid term,

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:50.883
depending on who
they're talking about.

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:53.319
But the movies were not intended
for children, alright?

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.055
-Yes, I guess that's
a good way to say it, yeah.

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:56.322
-They were not
intended for children.

00:00:56.322 --> 00:01:00.560
And the thing is, they were
also -- It's the very --

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.996
It's the very beginning
of the '70s,

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:07.466
so movies have exploded
because of the ratings board.

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:10.669
Movies have -- Just actually so
the audience knows,

00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:13.806
part of the thing is, before the
ratings board came out,

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:15.541
there was --
One, there was the Hays Code,

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:19.111
but the other thing about
before the ratings code is,

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:23.315
if you made a touchy movie,
be it sexuality or violence

00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:26.552
or any kind of thing like that,
you could be sued

00:01:26.552 --> 00:01:29.188
by any sheriff in
a jerk-water county

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:30.890
who said you broke
their county's obscenity laws.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.224
-Right.

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:35.128
-So the ratings board, you know,
once they came about --

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:38.298
that became the law of the land.

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:42.168
So, consequently,
movies just kind of exploded

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.537
when it came to
graphic subject matter,

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.773
like I say, either sexuality,
violence, or disturbing stories.

00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:49.742
-Do you remember
the first disturbing thing

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.211
you saw that
made an impact on you?

00:01:52.211 --> 00:01:56.882
-I saw -- There was
a movie about Isadora Duncan

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:59.552
that was directed, the British
director, Karel Reisz

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:00.953
called "Isadora,"

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:03.822
and it starred Vanessa Redgrave
playing Isadora Duncan.

00:02:03.822 --> 00:02:08.228
So, the ending is --
she's in the back of a roadster

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:11.497
and she's wearing this
long, flowing scarf

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.566
that's just hanging down.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:17.236
And -- "Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha!
Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha!"

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:18.671
They're driving around.

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.906
I think she even has a champagne
glass in her hand.

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:22.342
"Ha, ha, ha!"

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:24.577
And then the scarf gets caught
in the wheel of the roadster

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.313
and...

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:28.714
strangles her to death.

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:31.350
-And this was not a movie
where there was any sense

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.818
that this was going to be
the ending.

00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:33.953
-No.
-Not a horror film at all.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.188
-No, no, no, not at all.

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.989
In fact, I'm sure
that one of the reasons

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.792
it made such an impression is
I was so thoroughly bored

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:42.495
by the movie up
until that moment,

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:44.397
until that happened, alright?

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:46.432
I'm like...

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.002
You know,
and so, on the ride home,

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:50.636
my parents were driving me home.

00:02:50.636 --> 00:02:55.708
So I'm just full of questions
about dying by your scarf

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.076
getting caught
in the wheels of a car.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.144
-Yeah.

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:00.746
-And it's like
this major concern.

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:02.181
All of a sudden,
I'm so afraid of this.

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:07.886
And my mom says, "Quentin,
you have nothing to worry about.

00:03:07.886 --> 00:03:11.657
I would never, ever,
under any circumstances,

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:15.461
let you wear
a long, flowing scarf

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:18.298
in a convertible roadster."

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:19.598
Whew!

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:22.702
-Now, I feel like less
with movies, more with comedies,

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.103
my parents introduced me
to things

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.771
that my peers had
not been introduced to.

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:26.905
-Yeah, yeah.
But that makes sense, actually.

00:03:26.905 --> 00:03:28.474
-And then the teachers, though,

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:29.943
maybe they
catch onto this, right?

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:33.279
Were you sort of the person that
disseminated these adult films

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.746
and their plots
to your classmates?

00:03:34.746 --> 00:03:38.450
-No. There was this aspect --
Usually, one other kid

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.852
that was allowed to see
the contraband stuff.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.120
-Yeah.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.856
-There was this thing where
I'd go see a movie,

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:46.492
and then I'd go
back to school and, like,

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:49.661
spill the goods on this
wild contraband stuff.

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:51.698
And, usually, the kids --

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:53.999
Not always,
because I saw a lot of stuff,

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:56.002
but, oftentimes, they even knew
what the movie was

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:57.570
because they showed TV spots.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.338
-Yeah, so they'd know
the trailer, right.

00:03:59.338 --> 00:04:04.177
-One of the better times
as far as that was concerned was

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:07.746
my mother -- Again,
this is like, ah, the '70s.

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:09.148
They used to have
things like this.

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.851
All of a sudden,
out of the blue,

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.186
they would have --
United Artists --

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:15.254
not just like one theater.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:17.156
United Artists would, citywide,

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:19.725
do a big release of three
Woody Allen movies.

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:21.059
-Uh-huh.

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:24.597
-And, so, it was "Bananas"
and "Everything You Always

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.465
Wanted to Know About Sex
(But Were Afraid to Ask)"

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:27.799
and "Sleeper."

00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:30.769
And, so -- And my mom wasn't
even a Woody Allen fan.

00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:32.304
And, so, I asked her,
could we go,

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:34.106
like, on a "school night,"
no less,

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.641
and she goes, "Yeah."

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:37.443
I cuoldn't believe she said yes,
but she said yes.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.579
And, like,
the three Woody Allen movies,

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:42.215
and she doesn't like
Woody Allen, alright?

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:44.384
That was really nice of her.
-She's very -- Sounds lovely.

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:46.119
-She's really cool, alright?

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:52.524
And, so, we go to the movie,
and then there's everything --

00:04:52.524 --> 00:04:55.661
Again, imagining a child
watching this.

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:57.663
You're watching "Everything You
Always Wanted to Know About Sex

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.098
(But Were Afraid to Ask),"

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:03.469
and there's the monster-movie
parody about the giant boob

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.571
that's, you know,
going across the countryside.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.206
-Like "The Blob."
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:10.376
-But it's just a big -- It's
a big boob, a big tit, alright?

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:13.212
You know?
And it squirts milk, no less.

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:14.714
-Yep.

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:18.784
-Well, you can imagine how
I'm regaling the kids at school

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:24.523
the next -- "I saw a movie
that had a giant boob.

00:05:24.523 --> 00:05:28.594
And it drowned people with
milk."

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:31.096
In fact, I think the line
was, "The cream slowed him down,

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:34.566
and the milk killed him."

00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:36.468
-You -- One of
the great things is,

00:05:36.468 --> 00:05:38.203
you write about movies,
and not only do you write about

00:05:38.203 --> 00:05:40.372
"This is my take on the movie,
this is what I loved about it,"

00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:42.875
but you also sometimes have
a lot of knowledge

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:45.444
of how the movie was made,
choices that were --

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:48.013
casting and who the director was
and who the script was.

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:51.317
Did you find it was
difficult to figure out how to

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:53.319
combine those two things,
both your personal taste

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:56.389
and your personal experience
of knowing about the backstory?

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:57.890
-Well, that was actually
one of the fun things

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.860
about writing the book.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:06.899
It was as thing that made it
similar to me writing fiction,

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:08.233
where the thing about it is,
with fiction,

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:10.302
I have a really good idea
about where I'm going to go

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:12.904
with some of this stuff,
but at some point,

00:06:12.904 --> 00:06:16.075
I want the characters
to take it from me, you know,

00:06:16.075 --> 00:06:20.212
and start writing some of
the stuff itself

00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:22.749
so I'm actually almost,
like, dictating it.

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:25.184
And, to some degree,
when I got into

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.319
my own arguments
with myself on the page,

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.688
that started happening.

00:06:28.688 --> 00:06:32.057
But then one of the things was,
I hadn't thought that

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:35.127
I would be have -- I wouldn't
call what I do journalism,

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:37.063
but I didn't think I was
going to have that much

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:40.366
backing up, other than
my own general knowledge

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:41.800
of how a movie was made.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.769
But then, like, I'm doing
"The Getaway" chapter,

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:45.838
and I go, "Well, I'm actually
friends with Walter Hill,

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:47.139
and he wrote the script.

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:50.576
He was there for everything.
Let me give him a call."

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.545
And I give him a call,
and he just --

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.446
he's a wealth of information.

00:06:54.446 --> 00:06:56.849
Not only is -- You know,
he's a terrific writer.

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:58.617
He's proud of the movie,
but he's a great raconteur.

00:06:58.617 --> 00:06:59.685
And he was there.

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:02.588
He was there for
every moment of it.

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:04.756
And, so, he tells me
this amazing story

00:07:04.756 --> 00:07:06.858
that I didn't even really know.

00:07:06.858 --> 00:07:08.694
And then I was able to back
that up with this,

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.930
and he's the one that said,
"Why don't you give

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.399
Steve McQueen's ex-wife,
Neile McQueen, a call?

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:15.667
You don't really know
how important she was

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:17.103
to McQueen's success."

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:18.504
You know, and,
so, I give her a call,

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:23.375
and she's this amazing raconteur
and has a fantastic memory,

00:07:23.375 --> 00:07:26.378
you know, and tells me
all this stuff.

00:07:26.378 --> 00:07:28.781
And so it was
one of those situations

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:31.683
where if I didn't just
have a point,

00:07:31.683 --> 00:07:34.654
i.e. my own relationship
with a movie,

00:07:34.654 --> 00:07:37.089
or if I didn't want to
just do analysis,

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:39.625
I knew that was
another way to get in

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:42.261
was to just go through
like, a verbal history,

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:44.563
an oral history
of how the movie was made.

00:07:44.563 --> 00:07:46.998
-It's really -- Every sort of
section of the book

00:07:46.998 --> 00:07:48.734
has this really nice dimension
to it because of that.

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:50.603
I'm wondering,
as you write film criticism,

00:07:50.603 --> 00:07:52.271
what is your relationship
to the way

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:53.806
people write about your films?

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:56.776
Like, even when people don't
like things I've done,

00:07:56.776 --> 00:07:58.977
I always do appreciate
when they take the time to

00:07:58.977 --> 00:08:00.079
sort of drill down.

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.447
Is that how
you feel about critics?

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:03.115
Like, what is your --
Do you like reading reviews

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:04.316
of your work?

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:08.153
-Oh, yeah, no, I read them all.
I read them all.

00:08:08.153 --> 00:08:13.125
Look, I really know my movie
really well, alright, you know?

00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:18.030
So were there were a case that
somebody could shine a light on

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.466
me that I couldn't see
on my own --

00:08:20.466 --> 00:08:21.834
They might not like it.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:24.570
They might not appreciate
what I'm doing.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.271
But at the end of the day --
And this is

00:08:26.271 --> 00:08:29.008
an easy thing to say,
but, truly, most of the time,

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:31.109
well, I didn't really make
the movie for those people.

00:08:31.109 --> 00:08:32.411
You know,
I made it for somebody else.

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:34.146
-Yeah.

00:08:34.146 --> 00:08:36.982
-And -- But, also,
I've made movies where

00:08:36.982 --> 00:08:39.885
I've had fans that give
good reviews of my movies,

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:42.154
and they don't like this one.

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.189
Well, that doesn't mean
they're right either.

00:08:44.189 --> 00:08:50.129
But, also, it can just also
not be for you.

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:54.967
But I'm actually --
It's less of a situation of,

00:08:54.967 --> 00:08:57.436
"Oh, hey, they're going to
tell me something about myself

00:08:57.436 --> 00:08:58.537
I don't know."

00:08:58.537 --> 00:09:01.173
That can happen,
and that has happened.

00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:05.177
But it's more,
"Oh, this is the conversation

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:07.346
that's happening
about the movie."

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:10.483
And that's kind of important,
because there really only is

00:09:10.483 --> 00:09:11.684
a conversation about the movie
when it comes out.

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:12.952
-Yep.

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:15.654
-After that, it -- You know,
all those newspapers

00:09:15.654 --> 00:09:18.591
are in birdcages,
and no one remembers anything.

00:09:18.591 --> 00:09:23.129
And the perception of
the industry, of the fans,

00:09:23.129 --> 00:09:24.797
of everybody is kind of set.

00:09:24.797 --> 00:09:27.466
Now, it can change, usually,
over the course of time,

00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:29.735
but the conversation
about the movie,

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:32.505
good, bad, or indifferent,
is what's important.

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:34.873
-I think it's really special
that you wrote this book that,

00:09:34.873 --> 00:09:36.442
for the people who are going to
take the time to read this,

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:38.443
it's going to start
conversations about these movies

00:09:38.443 --> 00:09:40.846
that I think, otherwise,
for this generation,

00:09:40.846 --> 00:09:42.581
would forget.

00:09:42.581 --> 00:09:43.749
And so that's a cool thing
you did that,

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:45.083
and it's great to
see you, as always.

00:09:45.083 --> 00:09:46.385
Thanks so much for being here.
-Oh, my pleasure, man.

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:47.987
My pleasure.
-Quentin Tarantino.

00:09:47.987 --> 00:09:49.822
"Cinema Speculation" is out now.

00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:51.891
We'll be right back with
Emily Ratajkowski.

